"Material Resources, Productivity and the Environment (EN) (https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2015/02/material-resources-productivity-and-the-environment_g1g27386/9789264190504-en.pdf)\nciteturn267view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 176; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2015/02/material-resources-productivity-and-the-environment_g1g27386/9789264190504-en.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 8094\nL0@P0: OECD Green Growth Studies\nL1@P0: Material Resources,\nL2@P0: Productivity and the\nL3@P0-2: EnvironmentOECD Green Growth Studies\nL4@P2: Material Resources,\nL5@P2: Productivity\nL6@P2-3: and the EnvironmentThis work is published under the responsibility of the Secretary-General of the OECD. The\nL7@P3: opinions expressed and arguments employed herein do not necessarily reflect the official\nL8@P3: views of OECD member countries.\nL9@P3: This document and any map included herein are without prejudice to the status of or\nL10@P3: sovereignty over any territory, to the delimitation of international frontiers and boundaries\nL11@P3: and to the name of any territory, city or area.\nL12@P3: ISBN 978-92-64-19049-8 (print)\nL13@P3: ISBN 978-92-64-19050-4 (PDF)\nL14@P3: Series: OECD Green Growth Studies\nL15@P3: ISSN 2222-9515 (print)\nL16@P3: ISSN 2222-9523 (online)\nL17@P3: The statistical data for Israel are supplied by and under the responsibility of the relevant Israeli authorities. The use\nL18@P3: of such data by the OECD is without prejudice to the status of the Golan Heights, East Jerusalem and Israeli\nL19@P3: settlements in the West Bank under the terms of international law.\nL20@P3: Corrigenda to OECD publications may be found on line at: www.oecd.org/about/publishing/corrigenda.htm.\nL21@P3: © OECD 2015\nL22@P3: You can copy, download or print OECD content for your own use, and you can include excerpts from OECD publications, databases and\nL23@P3: multimedia products in your own documents, presentations, blogs, websites and teaching materials, provided that suitable\nL24@P3: acknowledgment of the source and copyright owner is given. All requests for public or commercial use and translation rights should be\nL25@P3: submitted to rights@oecd.org. Requests for permission to photocopy portions of this material for public or commercial use shall be\nL26@P3: addressed directly to the Copyright Clearance Center (CCC) at info@copyright.com or the Centre français d’exploitation du droit de copie\nL27@P3: (CFC) at contact@cfcopies.com.\nL28@P3: Please cite this publication as:\nL29@P3: OECD (2015), Material Resources, Productivity and the Environment, OECD Green Growth Studies, OECD\nL30@P3: Publishing, Paris.\nL31@P3-4: http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264190504-enPREFACE\nL32@P4: MATERIAL RESOURCES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015 3\nL33@P4: PREFACE\nL34@P4: Establishing a resource efficient economy is central to achieving green growth. It involves\nL35@P4: putting in place policies that improve resource productivity and that ensure a sustainable natural\nL36@P4: resource and materials management building on the principle of the 3Rs —reduce, reuse and\nL37@P4: recycle— and encouraging more sustainable consumption patterns. Better resource productivity can\nL38@P4: both help to improve the environment, by reducing the amount of resources that economic activity\nL39@P4: requires and diminishing the associated environmental burden, and help to sustain economic\nL40@P4: growth by securing adequate supplies of materials, investing in new technologies and innovation,\nL41@P4: and improving competitiveness.\nL42@P4: To be successful resource efficiency and material productivity policies need to be founded on a\nL43@P4: good understanding of the material basis of the economy, of international and national flows of\nL44@P4: materials, and of the factors that drive changes in natural resource use and material productivity\nL45@P4: over time, across countries and in the different sectors of the economy. Some natural resources,\nL46@P4: such as water, energy, forests, are monitored internationally, but information is insufficient to give\nL47@P4: an integrated view of how minerals, metals, or timber flow through the economy throughout their\nL48@P4: life cycle. In addition, little is known about how this affects the productivity of the economy and the\nL49@P4: quality of the environment.\nL50@P4: This report is a first step to fill some of these gaps. It describes the material basis of OECD\nL51@P4: economies and provides a factual analysis of material flows and resource productivity in OECD\nL52@P4: countries in a global context. It takes an economy-wide perspective and dwells upon major material\nL53@P4: categories such as metals, minerals, biomass and energy carriers. Some of the challenges and\nL54@P4: opportunities associated with selected materials and products that are internationally-significant,\nL55@P4: both in economic and environmental terms (aluminium, copper, iron and steel, paper, phosphate\nL56@P4: rock and rare earth elements) are also described. The report also highlights the most important\nL57@P4: information and knowledge gaps that need to be filled to effectively support resource efficiency and\nL58@P4: material productivity policies.\nL59@P4: Building on this report, we plan to both deepen our analysis of trade-related material flows\nL60@P4: and flows of secondary raw materials, the links with commodity prices and recycling markets, and\nL61@P4: the links with natural and anthropogenic stocks of materials. We will also review the environmental\nL62@P4: consequences of material resource use, as well as the economic and environmental opportunities\nL63@P4: provided by improved resource productivity. We will do so by working closely with national and\nL64@P4: international partners, in particular the International Resource Panel steered by UNEP.\nL65@P4: Simon Upton\nL66@P4-6: OECD Environment Director FOREWORD\nL67@P6: MATERIAL RESOURCES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015 5\nL68@P6: FOREWORD\nL69@P6: This report is part of the OECD programme of work on material flows and resource\nL70@P6: productivity that supports the implementation of the 2004 and 2008 OECD Council\nL71@P6: recommendations related to material flows and resource productivity. It contributes to OECD work\nL72@P6: on monitoring progress towards green growth and to the OECD project on measuring economic\nL73@P6: performance and social progress.\nL74@P6: A first version of this report was issued at the occasion of the Green Growth and Sustainale\nL75@P6: Development Forum in December 2012.\nL76@P6: Its elaboration has been drawing upon the OECD’s expertise with with environmental\nL77@P6: reporting, resource productivity and sustainable materials management. It benefitted from the\nL78@P6: financial support of Japan and from contributions by members of the OECD Working Parties on\nL79@P6: Environmental Information and on Resource Productivity and Waste. It has been drafted as a joint\nL80@P6: effort by a small group of experts from OECD countries led by the OECD Secretariat (Myriam\nL81@P6: Linster and Farah Huq). Task team members included: Mr Derry Allen (USEPA), Ms Kate Johnson\nL82@P6: and Mr. Jeff L. Doebrich (USGS), Ms Cheryl Beillard (Natural Resources Canada), Mr Pawel\nL83@P6: Kazmierzick (EEA), Mr Yuichi Moriguchi (Japan), Mr. John Atherton (BIAC), Mr Guido\nL84@P6: Sonnemann (UNEP), Mr Peter Borkey (OECD). Statistical and analytical support was provided by\nL85@P6: Pierpaolo Cazzola and Mauro Migotto.\nL86@P6: Part I of the report provides the economic and policy context for the report by outlining the\nL87@P6: main issues and challenges arising from global patterns and trends in material use. It highlights the\nL88@P6: linkages between production and consumption, economic growth, international trade, technological\nL89@P6: development and environmental issues.\nL90@P6: It further describes the material basis of the economy. It examines trends in the stocks and\nL91@P6: flows of materials worldwide and in OECD countries. It begins with an integrated analysis of trends\nL92@P6: in the extraction, consumption and trade of materials using economy-wide Material Flow Analysis\nL93@P6: (EW-MFA), distinguishing six groups of materials: 1) biomass for food and feed, 2) wood, 3) fossil\nL94@P6: energy carriers, 3) construction minerals, 5) industrial minerals, 6) metals and metal ores. It then\nL95@P6: looks at global and regional flows of waste and recycled materials and presents the latest state of the\nL96@P6: art on estimating material stocks, both natural and man-made.\nL97@P6: Part II of the report presents a series of factsheets on selected materials and products that are\nL98@P6: internationally-significant, in both economic and environmental terms: aluminium, copper, iron\nL99@P6-8: and steel, paper, phosphorus and rare earth elements. TABLE OF CONTENTS\nL100@P8: MATERIAL RESOURCES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015 7\nL101@P8: TABLE OF CONTENTS\nL102@P8: Executive summary .......................................................................................... 9\nL103@P8: Reader’s guide ................................................................................................. 11\nL104@P8: PART I: THE MATERIAL BASIS OF THE ECONOMY AND RESOURCE\nL105@P8: PRODUCTIVITY 19\nL106@P8: Chapter 1. Natural resources: The material basis of the economy .................... 21\nL107@P8: The role and functions of natural resources ....................................................................... 22\nL108@P8: Natural resources and natural capital ................................................................................ 23\nL109@P8: The resource cycle and the materials balance..................................................................... 25\nL110@P8: The environmental implications of resource use ................................................................ 28\nL111@P8: Chapter 2. An evolving economic context and new environmental challenges .. 35\nL112@P8: Rising demand for natural resources and commodities ..................................................... 36\nL113@P8: A changing geography of demand and supply ..................................................................... 37\nL114@P8: New environmental challenges and policy directions ........................................................ 40\nL115@P8: From waste to resources: closing the material cycle .................................................... 41\nL116@P8: Technology development and innovation ................................................................... 42\nL117@P8: Consumption patterns and consumer behaviour ....................................................... 43\nL118@P8: Chapter 3. Sustainable resource use and resource productivity .......................45\nL119@P8: The policy agenda ............................................................................................................... 46\nL120@P8: National and international initiatives ................................................................................. 48\nL121@P8: Improving knowledge and information for decision making ............................................. 53\nL122@P8: Measuring and analysing material flows .................................................................... 53\nL123@P8: Filling knowledge gaps and advancing the measurement .......................................... 56\nL124@P8: Chapter 4. The material basis of the global economy ....................................... 61\nL125@P8: Material extraction rates ..................................................................................................... 62\nL126@P8: Decoupling and material productivity ................................................................................ 66\nL127@P8: Chapter 5. The material basis of OECD economies .......................................... 69\nL128@P8: Trends in material use ........................................................................................................ 70\nL129@P8: Decoupling and resource productivity ................................................................................. 74\nL130@P8: Trends in material use by material group ........................................................................... 83\nL131@P8: Construction minerals ................................................................................................ 83\nL132@P8: Fossil energy carriers .................................................................................................. 84\nL133@P8: Biomass for food and feed ........................................................................................... 86\nL134@P8: Metals and metal ores ................................................................................................. 87\nL135@P8: Industrial minerals ..................................................................................................... 88\nL136@P8-9: Wood ........................................................................................................................... 89TABLE OF CONTENTS\nL137@P9: 8 MATERIAL RESOURCES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015\nL138@P9: Waste and secondary raw materials ................................................................................... 90\nL139@P9: Municipal solid waste .................................................................................................. 91\nL140@P9: Material recovery ........................................................................................................ 92\nL141@P9: International material flows ............................................................................................... 93\nL142@P9: Resource stocks .................................................................................................................... 97\nL143@P9: Natural stocks .............................................................................................................. 97\nL144@P9: Man-made stocks: urban mines .................................................................................. 99\nL145@P9: PART II: KEY MATERIAL RESOURCES FACTSHEETS 107\nL146@P9: Aluminium factsheet ..................................................................................... 108\nL147@P9: Copper factsheet ........................................................................................... 119\nL148@P9: Iron and steel factsheet ................................................................................. 130\nL149@P9: Paper factsheet .............................................................................................. 140\nL150@P9: Phosphorus factsheet .................................................................................... 149\nL151@P9: Rare earth elements factsheet ....................................................................... 158\nL152@P9-10: GLOSSARY 166EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\nL153@P10: MATERIAL RESOURCES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015 9\nL154@P10: EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\nL155@P10: Natural resources are fundamental for the economy and for well-being. They provide essential\nL156@P10: raw materials, water and other commodities, and are an important source of income and jobs. With\nL157@P10: land and ecosystems, they form our society’s natural capital.\nL158@P10: Establishing a resource efficient and sustainable economy is central to achieving green growth.\nL159@P10: It is the way to ensure adequate supplies of materials; to manage the environmental impacts\nL160@P10: associated with their extraction, processing, transport, use and disposal; and to make sure that natural\nL161@P10: resources are not degraded.\nL162@P10: To be successful resource efficiency policies need to be founded on a good understanding of the\nL163@P10: material basis of the economy, of international and national flows of materials, and of the factors that\nL164@P10: drive changes in resource use and productivity across time, countries and sectors. This report is a first\nL165@P10: step to provide some of this information.\nL166@P10: Key messages\nL167@P10: The last decades have witnessed unprecedented growth in demands for raw materials\nL168@P10: worldwide, driven in particular by the rapid industrialisation of emerging economies and continued\nL169@P10: high levels of material consumption in developed countries. At the same time, international\nL170@P10: commodity markets have expanded, with increasing international trade flows, and increasing mobility\nL171@P10: and fragmentation of production. This has been accompanied by increases in, and volatility of,\nL172@P10: commodity prices, and by growing competition for selected raw materials.\nL173@P10: The amount of materials extracted from natural resources and consumed\nL174@P10: worldwide doubled since 1980, an estimated ten-fold increase since 1900. It reached nearly\nL175@P10: 72 billion metric tonnes (Gt) in 2010, and is projected to reach 100 Gt by 2030.The annual per\nL176@P10: capita material consumption in OECD countries remains high, at about 60% above the\nL177@P10: world average. The average person living in an OECD country consumed roughly 46 kg of\nL178@P10: materials per day in 2011, including 10 kg of biomass, 18 kg of construction and industrial\nL179@P10: minerals, 13 kg of fossil energy carriers, and 5 kg of metals.\nL180@P10: Material consumption in OECD countries increased however more slowly than at the\nL181@P10: global level, and there are first signs of decoupling from economic growth and of\nL182@P10: improvements in material productivity. Today, OECD countries generate 50% more\nL183@P10: economic value per unit of material resources used than in 1990 and 30% more than in 2000.\nL184@P10: This is partly due to policy action: some countries managed to decrease material consumption\nL185@P10: while economic growth increased (absolute decoupling) with well-functioning waste\nL186@P10: management and extended producer responsibility schemes, and well-developed resource\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn267view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://worrydream.com/refs/NRC_1999_-_Funding_a_Revolution%2C_Government_Support_for_Computing_Research.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://worrydream.com/refs/NRC_1999_-_Funding_a_Revolution%2C_Government_Support_for_Computing_Research.pdf: (400) Content length is too large: 11047576\n"
